tasks.description:
Chooses the infrastructure that cuts the pipeline's fixed cost, on the evidence the analysis produced. docs/research/pipeline-cost-design.md compares candidates on the same criteria. Candidates to evaluate, and any others the evidence points to:
- a light path chosen by task risk and size;
- reference prose loaded by section on demand instead of whole documents;
- a per-task spawn brief, computed by a script, that replaces the reference reading;
- rules a script already enforces deleted from the prose;
- the reviewer gated by risk;
- a layout that keeps the prompt cache warm across stages.

Criteria for each candidate:
- predicted saving, computed from the measured stage costs and labelled a prediction;
- what the plugin guarantees that could weaken, and how that is held;
- maintenance cost;
- migration and COMPATIBILITY.md obligations;
- the benchmark run that would confirm or refute it.

The document recommends the structurally correct option. It also gives the implementation tasks, each with its own measurable target, so the next phase step can be planned from it.

Fixes review findings P117-R1 to P117-R11 of the cost phase's first review round.

Tools:
- stream-cost.py gains these cases, each shown red against the mutation the review named before it is trusted:
  - a modelUsage cache read that is off from the prefix identity by a known amount, which must print the DIFFERS verdict with that amount and never the agrees verdict;
  - a subagent that writes at the one-hour rate, which must carry the one-hour write into the rebuilt final request;
  - Read and Edit of the plan files, classed as plan work.
- Emitted Edit and Write bytes are classed by their target path, the same way tool results are.
- measure-context.py leaves out of the always-on listing any command or skill whose frontmatter sets disable-model-invocation, with a case for it.
- The analysis block that prints the listing is regenerated.

Documents:
- The design names that C3 needs the reviewer to file its return, and says how that write is held. It is one filing verb that can only write the reviewer's own return path, or 3(b) is chosen for the reviewer with the trade stated.
- The per-task request count is made to agree between the analysis and the design, the re-check after a stale stamp is described as one observation, and the predictions are given with both counts.
- The cost-model arithmetic is corrected and written out:
  - the double-counted re-read term;
  - the executor's read subtraction;
  - the reviewer-fork and C7 figures;
  - the early-reads term in the run line.
- T2's target table uses one quantity throughout, the one the ceiling gate measures.
- The confirming benchmark's protocol is said to land with the whole-feature benchmark phase.
- Every re-derivation command is pinned to the SHA its figures were taken at.
- These claims are restated to their basis:
  - the main-loop TTL is called unknown;
  - R8's risky arm is quoted accurately;
  - the TTL and billing assumption is listed with the model's assumptions;
  - the two-cause sentence in the analysis is marked as inference or decomposed.

The user set the cost target on 2026-10-07, in two parts.

Targets:
1. Per task: the plugin's task cycle may cost at most 2x what plain Claude Code spends on the same task, ideally 1.25x.
2. Per phase: planning, the phase review and the phase gate are budgeted separately as a phase overhead. That overhead has to pay for itself: each part of it is justified by what it catches, measured, or it is a candidate for removal.

The current design does not meet the per-task target. It predicts a one-task run falling from 1.7484 to 1.0047 USD against about 0.30 plain, which is about 3.3x. On the whole-feature benchmark the plugin arm averaged about ten times the plain arm (grade.txt of the nine valid sessions).

Where the cost sits (whole-C-3, Opus, grade.txt token lines, priced with the confirmed table):
- cache reads, 10.66M tokens: 2.13 USD;
- cache writes, mostly at the one-hour rate: 1.78 USD;
- output: 0.93 USD.

Cache reads are 5 percent of the input price, and they still dominate through volume: 61 main-loop requests, about 174k tokens each. Every new tool output in the main loop is written once at the one-hour rate and then re-read on every later request.

This task revises docs/research/pipeline-cost-design.md so that the recommended set is predicted to meet the per-task target. The phase overhead is stated separately, with its own budget and a value test.

Evidence:
- The whole-feature sessions, now priceable.
- Arm C's cost decomposed into:
  - planning;
  - the task cycle per task;
  - sign-off;
  - main-loop context carried per request, split by origin: reference prose, plugin script output, file reads, hand-backs;
  - the main-loop request count.
- What each phase-level step caught in the recorded sessions, against what the hidden tests and the grader show. This includes the hidden requirement that every arm missed.

Options to evaluate beside the earlier candidates:
- a script that drives the whole pipeline deterministically, so the model only writes code and answers named decisions;
- terse plugin script output by default;
- one executor per phase instead of one per task;
- fewer and cheaper main-loop requests;
- a smaller subagent start: omitClaudeMd, preloaded skills, and the cache TTL that an agent definition can set;
- a per-task review that is cheap enough to fit, or that moves to the phase;
- an enforced rule moved from an agent's tool list to a hook, where that keeps it enforced.

The document gives a ladder:
- what is reached with no change to any guarantee;
- then, for each guarantee change, the saving it adds, the guarantee affected, and how that guarantee would be held instead.

The recommendation is the structurally correct set that meets the per-task target. Guarantee changes it needs go to the user as decisions with figures. The implementation tasks are restated so that together they reach the target, each with its own measurable target and confirming session.

outcome.technical:
Revises docs/research/pipeline-cost-design.md against the user's target: a task at most 2x plain, ideally 1.25x, with the phase overhead budgeted and value-tested. All nine whole-feature sessions are priced with tools/stream-cost.py at HEAD, and arm C is split into planning, preflight, task cycle, sign-off, sign-off's fix task and close.

Measured:
- the task cycle costs 3.95-4.74x plain;
- the cycle's main loop is 69-70 percent of it, at 22-28 requests for three tasks, each reading 168k-191k tokens;
- reference prose and command bodies are 60-71 percent of what the cycle read;
- the /audit:phase body is injected again on every invocation;
- phase overhead is 2.91-3.35 USD per session, larger than the cycle;
- no phase-level step moved a graded result.

The prediction model main(k, P, W, O) = r(kP + W(k-1)/2) + wW + oO reproduces today's cycle main loop within 1.1 percent in all three sessions.

Rung 1, with no guarantee change, is a script-driven pipeline. The script runs every step that needs no judgement, hands briefs by file path, prints each rule at its step, asks judgements as named decisions, and takes four main-loop requests per task, with planning as one batch call and sign-off as one step. It is predicted at 1.64-1.83x plain per task and 3.17-3.57x arm A (2.06-2.32x arm B) per whole session. Each labelled estimate is doubled in a sensitivity check, and the worst session stays under 2.0.

G1 moves the per-task intent review into the phase review and is predicted at 1.09-1.30x per task. G2 gates it on a computed signal. Both go to the user, with G1 recommended. One caveat is stated: a single task on feature-C-1 is 2.16x at rung 1 and 1.81x with G1.

The phase overhead budget is 0.75 USD per phase, with a value test per step. Implementation tasks are restated for a new phase, each with an offline micro-test. The order is micro-tests, then one single-task probe (about 0.64), then the full benchmark (about 5.6).

Host behaviour measured once per session: the longest main-loop gap was 67-81 s, so one-hour writes bought nothing; a later dispatch of the same agent type read its start from cache; ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} is substituted in command bodies.

An instrument fault is recorded for T1: stream-cost.py sizes only tool_result user blocks, so an injected command body is booked as main-loop output. Totals are right and labels are wrong.
